>> title: Dopiaza
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: chicken, indian, onion, tomato, curry
>> counters: Curry House
>> aka: do piaza, dopiazah, do-piaza, dhansak's neighbour, chicken dopiaza, lamb dopiaza
>> pairs-with: onion-tomato-masala, naan, pilau-rice
>> servings: 4
>> time: 1 hr
>> step.1: fry 8 min, until charred at the edges, then lift out
>> step.2: sear 6 min in the same fat
>> step.3: simmer 15 min, covered
>> step.4: fold the second onions back in, simmer 5 min

Fry @red onions{2%medium}(cut in eighths through the root, layers left together) in @ghee{3%Tbs}(42 g) in a wide #pan{} over high heat ~fry{8%min}, until they are blackened at the edges and still have a bite in the middle, then lift them out and keep them.

Sear @boneless chicken thighs{1 1/2%lb}(680 g; cut in 2-in pieces) in the fat the onions came out of, adding @ghee{1%Tbs}(14 g) if the pan looks dry, ~sear{6%min}.

Simmer @onion-tomato masala{1 1/2%cup}(360 g; the base recipe), @ground coriander{2%tsp}, @ground cumin{1%tsp}, @garam masala{1%tsp}, @kosher salt{1 1/2%tsp} and @water{1/2%cup}(120 mL) with @&(~1)seared chicken{} covered ~simmer{15%min}.

Fold @&(~3)charred onion wedges{} back into @&(~1)curry{} with @cilantro{3%Tbs}(chopped) and ~simmer{5%min}, uncovered, so they warm through and stay wedges.

Do-piaza means two onions, and the two are the point: one lot cooked down to nothing in the base, one lot charred and folded back in at the end so you can still find them on the fork. Get them in too early and you have made a bhuna. Lamb is the older version and wants ninety minutes between the searing and the second onions.
